<speak>
<p>Let T of X have integer coefficients on a cycle of length two to the k, with k at least one. Suppose T times its star equals two to the power two t, times w squared, where t is a nonnegative integer and w is odd. We will prove that every opposite coefficient difference is divisible by two to the t. The coefficients need not be signs.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Choose a primitive root of unity xi of order two to the k, and let D be half that order. We now use a supplied cyclotomic input: the specialization of Lemma two point one. After allowing rational numbers with odd denominators, the first D powers, one, xi, through xi to the D minus one, form a basis.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>In this ring, powers of xi minus one measure divisibility through a valuation v, normalized by v of two equals one. This is an arithmetic measure, not complex magnitude. The supplied fact is: if the valuation of the sum of b j times xi to the j is at least t, and the b j are integers, then every b j is divisible by two to the t.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>The basis comes from the cyclotomic polynomial X to the D plus one. Replacing X by one plus Y gives an Eisenstein polynomial at two. Also, two is a unit times the D-th power of xi minus one. These standard properties are the input that lets us read divisibility coefficient by coefficient.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Now substitute X equal to xi. Star becomes complex conjugation, so T of xi times its conjugate equals two to the power two t, times w squared. Apply the valuation: multiplication becomes addition. The odd number w has valuation zero, so the right side has valuation two t.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>The two factors on the left have equal valuation. Conjugation sends xi minus one to negative xi inverse times xi minus one. That multiplier is a unit, so it does not change the valuation. Therefore twice the valuation of T of xi equals two t, and the valuation of T of xi equals t.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Finally use xi to the D equals negative one. Pair a j times xi to the j with the opposite term, a sub j plus D times xi to the j plus D. Replace the extra xi to the D by negative one. Their sum becomes a j minus a sub j plus D, times xi to the j. Do this for every opposite pair.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Now T of xi is written in our basis, with coefficients a j minus a sub j plus D. Its valuation is t. The supplied basis fact therefore makes every one of these differences divisible by two to the t. This proves our claim from the stated cyclotomic input.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For a hypothetical order sixteen row, take k equal to four, t equal to two, and w equal to one. Here the coefficients are signs. Their possible differences are negative two, zero, or two. Only zero is divisible by four, so every entry equals the entry eight positions away. The eighth cyclic shift would repeat the row. Its dot product with the original would be sixteen, instead of the required zero.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>This illustration excludes order sixteen. In the general descent, the coefficients may be larger odd integers. We keep the divisibility result, which will let the folding step compare and average opposite coefficients.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
